{
  "id": "COMPOUND:22787 - PROTEIN:5867",
  "verbalization": "Khrinone C is shown to interact with CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, a protein that is targeted by multiple compounds, including (3aR,6S,6aR)-3,6-bis(3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan and 6'-O-Z-Para-Methoxycinnamoylharpagide. All these compounds have a final score of 0.5352, suggesting a plausible biological relationship. The shared target of these compounds with Khrinone C implies that the drug may exert its effects through a common mechanism, possibly by inhibiting the sialyltransferase enzyme. This enzyme is involved in the synthesis of sialic acid, which is critical for cell surface glycosylation and has been implicated in various cellular processes, including immune response and cell adhesion. The evidence suggests that Khrinone C may act through this enzyme, potentially modulating cellular functions in HaCaT cells, which are a human keratinocyte cell line. The integration of multiple pathways and the consistent targeting of the same enzyme across different compounds support a mechanistic link between Khrinone C and HaCaT, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact mode of action and therapeutic potential.",
